  "address": null,
  "summary": "Planned quieter park at the south-east tip of the site, set apart from Block 9's more social uses: natural planted areas, informal discovery play, and casual seating and picnicking, with a Bay overlook on the footprint of a decommissioned power plant intake structure and a section of Blue Greenway for a future connection toward Warm Water Cove, to be completed by others (Design for Development Section 4.20). The Power Station website calls it a place set 'at the tip of the site'.",
  "summary_sources": [
   "src-d4d",
   "src-dpps"
  ],
  "shape": {
   "kind": "approximate",
   "parcel": null,
   "display": "fill",
   "display_reason": null,
   "notes": {
    "accuracy_caveat": "APPROXIMATE -- the landform was read off satellite imagery to a few metres, and the strip around it comes from earlier approximate drawings (the Draft EIR's Port sub-area figure and the Waterfront Park trace), so treat its edges as indicative to roughly 10-20 m. The Design for Development's Figures 4.16.1 and 4.20.1 draw The Point as a rounded park with a rock edge; this shape does not reproduce that drawing. No source read gives The Point's own acreage, so the area here is only the area of this shape.",
    "digitized_from": "Traced by Pier & Point from Esri World Imagery (zoom 20) against a 10 m grid in UTM zone 10N (EPSG:26910): the rounded, rock-edged landform at the south-east tip of the site, east of the Spreckels warehouse, plus the shoreline strip around it that the earlier Waterfront Park and Prequel Park drawings placed there. The Design for Development labels it (2) 'The Point' on Figure 4.16.1 (printed p.93) and gives it Section 4.20 and Figure 4.20.1; the Power Station website describes it as 'set at the tip of the site'.",
    "why_no_real_parcel": "No Assessor lot of its own: DataSF's active lot 4232010 (401 23rd St, PDR-1-G zoning, 44,309 m2) lies under the Spreckels warehouse and the ground west of it, and about 10% of this shape (356 m2) falls inside it; the rest lies outside every active lot (checked against DataSF Parcels, 2026-10-11).",
    "regulatory_context": "Open space (4) in the Design for Development's Waterfront Open Spaces: Section 4.20 'The Point'. The Power Station website's Open Space page says: 'The Point marks the meeting of land, water, and wind at the edge of Power Station. Set at the tip of the site with uninterrupted views of the bay'."
   }
